WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.880
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.440
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.160
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.080
In other words,

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.080
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.000
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.160
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.920
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.800
Today we do the math on how many
fewer calories we have to eat every day

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.520
to lose one pound of body fat.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.600
The first surgical attempt at
body sculpting was in 1921,

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.360
with a dancer wanting to improve
the shape of her ankles.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.200
The surgeon apparently
scraped away too much tissue

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.760
and tied the stitches too tight,
resulting in necrosis, amputation,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.080
and the first recorded malpractice
suit in the history of plastic surgery.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:58.640
Today's liposuction is much safer—
only killing about 1 in 5,000 patients,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.720
mostly from unknown causes—
throwing a clot off into your lung,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.160
or perforations of
your internal organs.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.920
Liposuction currently reigns as
the most popular cosmetic surgery

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.680
in the world, and its effects
are indeed, only cosmetic.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.720
A study published in the New
England Journal of Medicine

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.720
assessed obese women before and
after having about 20 pounds of fat

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.560
sucked out of their bodies,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.240
resulting in nearly a 20%
drop in their total body fat.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.400
Normally, lose even just 5-10%
of your body weight in fat,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.000
and you get significant improvements
in blood pressure, blood sugars,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.480
inflammation, cholesterol,
and triglycerides,

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.720
but liposuction sucks: none
of those benefits materialized

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.600
even after the massive liposuction.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:52.320
This suggests subcutaneous fat, the
fat under our skin, is not the problem.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.520
The metabolic insults of obesity
arise from the visceral fat,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.440
the fat surrounding or even
infiltrating our internal organs

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.560
like the fat marbling
our muscles and liver.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.840
The way you lose that fat,
the dangerous fat,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.040
is to take in fewer
calories than you burn.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:14.080
Anyone who's seen The Biggest
Loser shows knows with enough

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.280
calorie restriction and exercise
hundreds of pounds can be lost.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.640
Similarly, there are cases in the medical
literature of what some refer to as

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.680
"super obesity" in which individuals
lost up to 374 pounds, largely

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.720
on their own without professional
help and kept it off for years.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.280
The guy lost about 20 pounds
a month cycling two hours a day

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.400
and reducing intake to 800 calories
a day, which is down around

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.400
what some prisoners were getting at
concentration camps in World War II.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.600
Perhaps America's most
celebrated TV weight loss

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.760
was when Oprah pulled
out a wagon full of fat,

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.040
representing the 67 pounds she
lost on a very-low-calorie diet.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:03.280
How many calories did she have to
cut to achieve that within four months?

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.760
Consult leading nutrition textbooks,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.760
or refer to trusted authorities
like the Mayo Clinic, and you'll

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.520
learn the simple weight loss rule: one
pound of fat is equal to 3,500 calories.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.400
Quoting from the Journal of the
American Medical Association:

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.320
A total of 3500 calories
equals 1 pound of body weight.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.360
This means if you decrease your
intake by 500 calories daily,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.680
you will lose 1 pound per
week. 500 calories per day

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.800
x 7 days = 3500 calories,
so 1 pound of body fat.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.480
The simple weight loss
rule that's simply not true.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:43.680
The 3,500-calorie rule can be
traced back to a paper in 1958

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.160
that just noted that since fatty tissue
on the human body is 87% fat,

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.160
a pound of body fat would have
about 395 grams of pure fat.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.880
Multiply that by nine calories
per gram of fat gives you that

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.640
3,500 calories per
pound approximation.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.920
The fatal flaw that leads to
dramatically exaggerated

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.040
weight loss predictions
is that the 3,500 rule fails

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.840
to take into account the fact
that changes in the Calories-In

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.000
side of the energy-balance
equation automatically lead to

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.520
changes in Calories Out,
for example the slowing

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.960
of metabolic rate that
accompanies weight loss

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.080
known as metabolic adaptation.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.440
That's one of the reasons
weight loss plateaus.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.680
For example, imagine a 30-year-old
sedentary woman of average height

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.840
who weighs 150 pounds.
According to the 3,500-calorie rule,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.280
if she cuts 500 calories out of her
daily diet, she'd lose a pound a week,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:48.040
or 52 pounds a year. In three
years then, she would vanish.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:54.640
She'd go from 150 pounds to negative 6.
Obviously, that doesn't happen.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.560
What would happen is that in the first
year instead of losing 52 pounds,

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.000
she'd likely only lose 32 pounds

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.800
and then, after a total of three years,
stabilize at about 100 pounds.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.560
This is because it takes fewer
calories to exist as a thin person.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.040
Part of it is simple mechanics,

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.880
in the same way a Hummer requires
more fuel than a compact car---

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.840
think how much more effort it
would take to just get out of a chair,

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.880
walk across the room, or climb a few
stairs carrying a 50-pound backpack.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.480
That's no lighter than carrying
50 pounds in the front.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.400
Even when you're lying
at rest, sound asleep,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.280
there's simply less of your body
to maintain as we lose weight.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.760
Every pound of fat tissue lost may
mean one less mile of blood vessels

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.480
your body has to pump
blood through every minute.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.560
So the basic upkeep and movement
of thinner bodies takes fewer calories.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:54.600
So as you lose weight by eating
less, you end up needing less.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.040
That's what the 3,500-calorie
rule doesn't take into account.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.200
Or imagine it the other way.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.760
A 200-pound man starts eating
500 more calories a day.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.520
That's like a large soda
or two doughnuts.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.080
According to the 3,500-calories
rule, in 10 years he'd weigh

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:13.560
more than 700 pounds!

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.600
That doesn't happen
because the heavier he is,

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.360
the more calories he
burns just existing.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.440
If you're a hundred pounds overweight,
that's like the skinny person inside you

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.240
trying to walk around balancing
13 gallons of oil at all times,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.960
or lugging around a sack containing
400 sticks of butter wherever you go.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.360
It takes about two doughnuts worth of
extra energy just to live at 250 pounds,

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:42.720
and so that's where he'd
plateau out if he kept it up.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.040
So weight gain or weight loss, given
a certain calorie excess or deficit,

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:53.000
is a curve that flattens out over time
rather than a straight line up or down.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.600
Nevertheless, the 3500-calorie
rule continues to crop up,

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.880
even in obesity journals.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.160
Public health researchers used it
to calculate how many pounds

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:07.760
children might lose every year if,
for example, fast food kid's meals

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.560
swapped in apple slices
instead of French fries.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:19.000
They figured two meals a week could
add up to about four pounds a year.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:23.200
The actual difference—National
Restaurant Association-funded

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.920
researchers were no doubt
delighted to point out—

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.440
would probably add less
than half a pound,10 times

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:34.360
less than the 3500-calorie
rule would predict.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:38.280
The original article was
subsequently retracted.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.160
In our next story,

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.400
we try to understand the metabolic
and behavioral adaptations

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.680
that slow weight loss.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:50.160
We have millions of years of evolution
hard-wiring us to survive scarcity,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.880
compensatory survival mechanisms our
body uses to defend against weight loss.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:08:00.680
So when we start losing weight, we
may unconsciously start moving less

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.400
as a behavioral adaptation
to conserve energy.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.920
There are metabolic adaptations as
well. Our metabolism slows down.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.040
Every pound of weight loss may
reduce our resting metabolic rate

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:19.720
by seven calories a day. This may only
translate to a few percent differences

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:26.400
for most, but can rapidly snowball for
those who achieve massive weight loss.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.680
During one session, some of
The Biggest Loser contestants

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.400
famously had their
metabolic rates tracked.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.120
Above and beyond the hundreds of
calories less it takes to just exist

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.440
100+ pounds lighter, by the end of
the season their metabolic rates

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.280
slowed by an extra
500 calories a day.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:49.080
The mindblower was that six
years later they were retested

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:54.120
and still had the 500-calorie-a-day
handicap. So the contestants had to cut

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.240
500 calories more than anyone else their
size to maintain the same weight loss.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.800
No wonder the bulk of
their weight was regained.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.320
Most remained at least 10% lower
than their starting weight, though,

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:13.800
and even a 7% drop has been shown
to cut diabetes rates about in half.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.600
Still, the metabolic slowing means
you have to work that much harder

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.440
than everyone else
just to stay in place.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.840
Analyzing four seasons of The
Biggest Loser, minute-by-minute,

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:31.080
researchers noted that 85% of the
focus was on exercise rather than diet,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.760
though the exercise component accounted
for less than half of the weight loss.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:41.040
Even six years after their season ended,
the contestants had been maintaining

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.160
an hour of daily, vigorous exercise,
yet still regained most of the weight.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.280
Why? Because they
started eating more.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.680
They could have
cut their exercise

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.240
to just 20 minutes a
day and still maintained

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.280
100% of their initial weight
loss if they would have just

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.840
been able to keep their intake
to under 3,000 calories a day.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.160
That may not sound like
much of a challenge,

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:10.400
but weight loss doesn't just slow your
metabolism; it boosts your appetite.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.280
If it were just a matter of your weight
settling at the point at which

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.320
your reduced calorie intake matches
your reduced calorie output,

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:20.640
it would take years for your
weight loss to plateau.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.120
Instead, it often happens
within six to eight months.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:26.800
You may know the drill:

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.120
Start the diet, stick to the diet,
and then weight loss stalls

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:34.600
six months later.
What happened?

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:38.880
Don't blame your metabolism;
that just plays a small part.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.240
What happened is that you likely
actually stopped sticking to your diet

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.280
because your appetite went on
a rampage. Let's break it down.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.600
If you cut 800 calories
out of your daily diet—

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.240
2600 calories a day
down to 1800—

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.200
and your weight loss stalls after six
months, then what happened is

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:03.960
that at the end of the first month you
think you're still cutting 800 calories,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:08.760
but you may actually only be
down about 600 calories a day.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.040
By month two you're
only down about 500,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:16.600
month three, 300, and by month
six you're only eating 200 calories

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.400
less than before
you went on the diet.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:23.600
In other words, you inadvertently
suffered an exponential increase

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:26.960
in calorie intake over
those six months.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.280
Yet you may not even realize
it, because by that time

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:33.680
your body may have ramped
your appetite up 600 calories.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:37.640
So, it still feels as if you are
eating 800 calories less,

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.200
but it's actually only 200.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.640
Since an 800 calorie drop in
intake may slow your metabolism

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.960
and physical activity
about 200 calories a day,

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.840
with no difference between Calories In
and Calories Out at six months,

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:55.840
no wonder your weight loss
grinds to a complete halt.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.160
The slow upward drift in
calorie intake on a new diet

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:02.920
is not because you got lazy.
Once your appetite is boosted by

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:08.120
600 calories after dieting for a while,
eating 200 calories less at the end

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:12.240
is as hard as eating 800 calories
less at the beginning.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:15.040
So you can maintain the same
disciplined level of willpower

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:19.200
and self-control and still
end up stagnating.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:24.720
To prevent this from happening, you
need to maintain the calorie deficit.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.920
How is that possible in the
face of a ravenous appetite?

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.960
Hunger is a biological drive.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.840
Asking someone to eat
smaller portions

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:36.800
is like asking someone
to take fewer breaths.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:42.000
You can white-knuckle it for a bit,
but eventually nature wins out.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.000
That's why I wrote
How Not to Diet.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.680
There are foods that can counter
the slowing of our metabolism

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.240
and suppress our appetite,

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:53.960
ways of eating to counter
the behavioral adaptation

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:59.000
and even eat more food
yet still lose weight.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.440
Due to the metabolic slowing
and increased appetite

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.360
that accompanies weight loss,
sustained weight loss requires

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:09.480
a persistent calorie deficit
of 300 to 500 calories a day.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.600
This can be accomplished
without reducing portion sizes

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:17.320
simply by lowering the
calorie density of meals.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:19.960
This can result in the rare
combination of weight loss

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:25.031
with both an increase in quality and
even quantity of food consumed.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.600
The bottom line is that
sustainable weight loss

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.680
is not about eating less food;
it's about eating better food.

